{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":1.0,"text":"In 1731, a Spanish officer sliced off a British captain's ear, and that one ear started a war. The captain was Robert Jenkins, and his little ship was stopped off the coast of Cuba. The Spaniards searched every corner and found nothing. So the angry Spanish officer drew his sword and cut off Jenkins' ear. Then he sneered, \"Go tell your king. He's next.\" Jenkins did not throw the ear away. He pickled it in a jar and carried it home to London. Seven years later, in 1738, he walked into Parliament and held the ear up high. The lords gasped, and the whole country screamed for revenge. [sighs] The Prime Minister, Robert Walpole, did not want a war, but the crowds were so loud that in 1739, Britain declared war on Spain, [laughs] and they named it after the ear, the War of Jenkins' Ear. Admiral Vernon sailed to the Caribbean, and in 1741, he attacked the city of Cartagena with almost 200 ships. But Spain had Blas de Lezo, a tough old admiral with one eye, one arm, and one leg. And with just a few ships and a few thousand men, he crushed the biggest fleet Britain had ever sent. Thousands of British sailors died, most of them from disease. The war dragged on and merged into an even bigger war that lasted until 1748, all because of one little ear. So if you like history where a single ear shakes up the world, follow for more stories like this.","words":[{"text":"In","start":0.06,"end":0.22,"type":"word","logprob":-0.0009115354623645544},{"text":" ","start":0.22,"end":0.28,"type":"spacing","logprob":-6.794906312279636e-6},{"text":"1731,","start":0.28,"end":1.46,"type":"word","logprob":-7.152464496584798e-6},{"text":" ","start":1.46,"end":1.68,"type":"spacing","logprob":-1.0728830375228426e-6},{"text":"a","start":1.68,"end":1.76,"type":"word","logprob":-1.0728830375228426e-6},{"text":" ","start":1.76,"end":1.8,"type":"spacing","logprob":-1.9073468138230965e-6},{"text":"Spanish","start":1.8,"end":2.2,"type":"word","log